;;; tur/region -- a declared lifetime bracket, independent of the trail.
;;;
;;; `with-region` runs a thunk inside an arena generation: allocations made
;;; while it runs are reclaimed in one O(slabs) rewind when it returns, PROVIDED
;;; the compiler can prove the returned value does not transitively reach any
;;; value allocated inside. When it cannot prove that, the generation is retired
;;; instead of rewound -- byte-for-byte what a build without the feature does --
;;; so refusing costs a saving, never correctness.
;;;
;;; This is the LIFETIME half of what `bt-scope` (stdlib/trail.tur) does, split
;;; out so a program that wants the reclamation but has no backtracking does not
;;; have to reach for a search primitive to get it. The full picture:
;;;
;;;   region only          `with-region`                 (this file)
;;;   trail level only     `bt-mark` / `bt-undo-to!`      (stdlib/trail.tur)
;;;   both                 `bt-scope`                     (stdlib/trail.tur)
;;;   neither              plain code
;;;
;;; The region boundary is a CODEGEN decision keyed on the call to
;;; `with-region`, not on anything this body does -- the body is a bare
;;; forwarder. The emitter opens a generation around the call and rewinds (or
;;; retires) it after -- on by default since 2026-09-05; under `TUR_REGIONS=0`
;;; (the bisection hatch) `with-region` is an ordinary identity call and the
;;; whole file is inert.
;;;
;;; See docs/upcoming/regions-plan.md and docs/guides/gc-guide.md.
;;;
;;; Since: RM3 (prototype 2026-09-04; on by default since 2026-09-05)
;; Declared-lifetime region bracket (reclamation-plan RM3 / regions-plan).

;;; with-region -- run `body` inside an arena generation and reclaim it after.
;;;
;;; The lifetime-only bracket: unlike `bt-scope` it pushes NO trail level, so it
;;; carries no `#fx{Bt}` and undoes no mutation -- it only bounds a generation.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   body -- a thunk. Taken ^fat, so a capturing lambda works -- which is the
;;;           only interesting case, since the body has to build the values
;;;           whose lifetime this bounds.
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   Whatever `body` returned. The VALUE survives; anything it allocated inside
;;;   that the value does not reach is reclaimed. Returning something that points
;;;   into reclaimed region memory is refused at compile time by declining to
;;;   rewind (the value's type is walked); a genuinely escaping pointer the walk
;;;   cannot see is caught at run time and blocks the rewind. Neither is a memory
;;;   safety hole -- the failure mode is "no saving", not a stale read.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (with-region (fn [] (sum-of (build-scratch 100))))  ; scratch reclaimed
;;;
;;; Since: RM3
(defn with-region [A] [^fat body : (fn [] A)] : A (body))
